{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli** also known as Raymond of Antioch was a prominent Crusader leader of the 12th century. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem making him a key figure within the Crusader states in the Levant.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1: Early Life and Family Background*\nRaymond III grew up in a family deeply involved in the Crusades. His father Raymond II was one of the most powerful rulers among the Crusader states. His mother Hodierna who was the sister of King Fulk of Jerusalem added to his noble lineage. This background provided Raymond III with strong political connections and military training from an early age.\n\nRaymond succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was only about 12 years old. Due to his young age his mother Hodierna served as regent until he came of age. Despite his youth Raymond III was quickly recognized as a capable leader and proved himself in various military engagements.\n\n*Highlighted section part 2: Military and Political Career*\nRaymond III played a pivotal role in the political and military landscape of the Crusader states. He was known for his diplomacy and military acumen which helped him maintain alliances and defend his county against threats. One of his earliest significant actions was his participation in the defense of the Crusader states against Nur ad-Din a powerful Muslim leader.\n\nIn 1164 Raymond married Eschiva of Bures who was the widow of Joscelin II Count of Edessa. This marriage significantly strengthened Raymond’s position within the Crusader states. He also formed alliances with other prominent figures such as Amalric I of Jerusalem and Bohemond III of Antioch.\n\nRaymond III is particularly known for his resistance against Muslim forces led by Saladin who became a major threat to the Crusader states. In 1170 Raymond participated in the defense of Harim against Nur ad-Din’s forces and later played a crucial role in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he supported Guy of Lusignan against Saladin.\n\n*Highlighted section part 3: Later Years and Legacy*\nIn the later years of his life Raymond faced increasing pressures from Saladin’s expanding influence. Despite several military setbacks Raymond III continued to resist Saladin’s advances. His efforts to maintain the independence of the County of Tripoli were crucial in prolonging the existence of the Crusader states.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 possibly due to injuries sustained in a battle. His death marked a turning point as the Crusader states began to face significant losses in the subsequent years particularly with the fall of Jerusalem in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin.\n\nRaymond III’s legacy lies in his contributions to the Crusader states his diplomatic skills and his military resistance against overwhelming forces. He was respected by both his contemporaries and historians for his leadership and dedication to defending the Crusader possessions in the Levant. His life exemplifies the challenges and complexities faced by the Crusader rulers in their struggle to maintain their presence in the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -3.568530797958374}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.7393481135368347}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00014280252798926085}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.007169117219746113}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.45276087522506714}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0006429274217225611}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.013922948390245438}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -0.10496137291193008}, {"token": " also", "logprob": -1.3165831565856934}, {"token": " known", "logprob": -0.08877769857645035}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.021637331694364548}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.5272077322006226}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.38218915